 "doc": "Meat Rack bundles round 2 (blender/meatbundles_r2.py): one recipe per item prefab, the game's OWN item model plus our cord, now ONE big piece per hook (kind single), a string of 2-3 one under the other for small pieces (kind stack, partial stacks show the top ones first), or two flat fish back to back (kind pair). Same schema as round 1. Hook space: origin = the hook point = centre of the rack's horizontal hook bar where the cord hangs (_hooks_meatrack_r4 lists them per r4 bundle unit, Unity prefab space). Unity axes: x across the rail, y up, z front (ux=-bx, uy=bz, uz=-by from Blender). Per copy: the item model in ItemVisual's item space (prefab root space without the root's own scale, already turned 180 degrees about up) gets the uniform `scale` (absolute, on the prefab's native size), then rot_quat_xyzw, and its ROOT goes to `offset`: copy matrix = T(hook) R(face) T(offset) R(rot) S(scale). Every item here has root scale 1, so scale = x_true_size, the fraction of the item's true size in the world; true_longest_m and item_longest_m give the longest bounds side at 1 and as hung. bounds_center is where the item's (rip) bounds centre lands. Fill: the first k copies for a partial stack; the cord is drawn whole. cord_obj is in the same folder: our own mesh (forward -Z, up Y, load with flipX like the frames), UVs on meatbundles_atlas.png (v 0.5-1 cord hemp, v 0-0.5 binding hemp), not tinted. Our strands start inside the frame's knot, 0.095 m below the hook point. Every bundle stays in the hook-space envelope of _checks (top 0.12 m and bottom 0.67 m below the hook point, 0.075 m behind to 0.042-0.095 m in front of the cord) and clear of its own copy 0.194 m along the rail, so a full rack has no two meshes touching (checked: _checks). Back-row hooks (faces=back): turn the whole bundle (copies and cord) 180 degrees about up through the hook point. Eggs never hang (the mod keeps them in the bins) and have no recipe. _generic, _fish and _not_ripped are documentation: the mod skips keys starting with '_'."
}